{"conjecture": "As systems evolve, the set of reachable behavioral envelope shrinks relative to total code because refactorings gate new paths behind feature flags, migration shims, and backward-compatibility layers that later become unreachable but are never deleted. Each such path was individually reviewed and tested, so it persists as a plausible execution route in developers' mental models, inflating the perceived behavioral space and forcing future changes to reason about paths that can no longer fire. The system becomes harder to change not because live behavior grows complex, but because the ratio of reasoning-required surface to actually-reachable surface diverges over time, and no existing tooling surfaces this divergence at change time."}
